issues.body:
### What do you want to do?

## Summary

When the Windows file tier reads unbuffered (chosen at load when the file cache cannot keep the experts, or forced with `STRATA_UNBUFFERED_LOAD=1`, which both arms below set), every expert outside RAM and VRAM is read with `FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING` once `drop_mapping()` closes the views. On my machine (RX 7800 XT 16 GB, 64 GB RAM, Windows 11) I tried an opt-in change
that keeps the unbuffered handles for the initial load and then reopens `experts.bin` (or the GGUF shards) with
`FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED` only, so later expert reads can be served by the Windows file cache.

Same binary, same day, only this switch changed, `STRATA_UNBUFFERED_LOAD=1` in both arms (the unset and `=0` cases are not part of this A/B; with `=0` an earlier build of the same tree at 19 GiB stopped on my 4 GiB free-RAM guard during the first 60K request), 60,047-token fresh prompt (0 reused), three runs each:

| | prompt tok/s | decode tok/s | TTFT s | total s |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| `STRATA_R6_FILE_READ_CACHE=0` | 1,399.8 (1,383.3–1,403.8) | 28.5 (24.6–28.9) | 43.08 | 56.19 |
| `STRATA_R6_FILE_READ_CACHE=1` | 1,476.4 (1,462.1–1,484.4) | 36.0 (32.2–36.3) | 40.74 | 51.20 |
| change | +5.5% | **+26.3%** | −5.4% | −8.9% |

The generated text was identical pair by pair (345 / 381 / 340 tokens). Physical SSD reads fell from 947.9 to
306.7 MiB/s while reading the prompt and from 678.4 to 24.7 MiB/s while decoding, while the engine's requested read rate did not drop (947 → 987 and 678 → 818 MiB/s). A real coding-agent run (6 tasks × 3) went from a median
of 61.45 s to 50.31 s over the 18 runs, 18/18 functional in both.

Full report, per-run data and launch configs: the community benchmark folder
`bench/results/2026-10-18-community-rx-7800-xt-windows/` (PR #3057).

This looks like the Windows counterpart of #1194 (Linux: cache vs `O_DIRECT` on the file tier, fixed for Linux in
0.1.40.2). It also touches the question in #2344, where the buffered path made prompt reading slower on some Linux
machines; here the prompt got slightly faster, but this is overlapped ReadFile through the Windows cache on one Windows machine, and I did not measure page faults.

## What the patch does

`r6-windows-cached-readfile.patch`, against `v0.1.44.1`, 57 added lines, one function
(`FileExpertSource::drop_mapping`, `_WIN32` branch). `drop_mapping` on today's `main` is the same as in 0.1.44.1.

- Off unless `STRATA_R6_FILE_READ_CACHE=1` (the name is from my experiment; any name is fine). With it unset the code
  path is unchanged.
- Runs only after the final view, section and cached handle are closed, so the initial RAM/GPU fill still reads
  unbuffered. `open_direct()` and the startup unbuffered path are not touched.
- Before switching, it reads three blobs (first, middle and last layer/expert) with the original unbuffered handles,
  reopens every file with `FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED` only, reads the same three blobs twice and compares bytes. If any open
  or compare fails, it closes the new handles and restores the original ones. The result is written to the startup log
  line (`R6 Windows cached ReadFile active; ... byte-probe PASS (3 blobs x 2 reads)`).
- The existing `read_direct()` overlapped reads are reused unchanged.

## Caveats

- **The measured binary is a private build**: 0.1.44.1 plus other local changes (a compact MMQ buffer, a persistent fetch team, CPU/IO overlap, @vizdatom's layer-major prefill from #1670 ported to this tree, and libratechw's opt-in HIP prompt-attention patches, not enabled in these runs; listed in the report). The A/B isolates this switch inside that
  build, but I have **not** measured the patch alone on top of a clean 0.1.44.1 or `main`.
- I built and ran it with HIP on Windows only. The change is inside `#if defined(_WIN32)` and has no GPU-backend code,
  but I did not build CUDA or SYCL.
- One machine: RX 7800 XT 16 GB, Ryzen 5 9600X, 64 GB DDR5, NVMe, expert RAM budget 19 GiB, Flash-Next IQ3_XXS,
  64K context, int8 KV. With 64 GB of RAM there is room for the file cache; a machine with less RAM may behave
  differently (the file cache competes with the resident arena and the commit limit).
- OFF ran before ON and the OS file cache was not flushed between arms, so this is not a cold-SSD comparison.
- MTP GPU fence waits still happen with the change (1 in the ON coding runs, 3 in the OFF runs); I do not claim it fixes
  them.

## Question

Would an opt-in switch like this be acceptable for Windows, or would you rather fold it into the existing
cache-vs-unbuffered decision (as #1194 did for Linux)? If useful, I can open a PR against current `main` with the patch
alone and re-measure it on a clean build first.

## Patch

```diff
diff --git a/src/core/expert_source.cpp b/src/core/expert_source.cpp
index 4878c11..1634a65 100644
--- a/src/core/expert_source.cpp
+++ b/src/core/expert_source.cpp
@@ -1723,6 +1723,61 @@ bool FileExpertSource::recheck_unbuffered(std::string& why) {
 bool FileExpertSource::drop_mapping(std::string& why) {
 #if defined(_WIN32)
     if (direct_.empty()) { why = "the reads are not unbuffered"; return false; }
+    // R6 opt-in, called only AFTER the final view/section/handle is closed.
+    // open_direct and begin/end_startup_unbuffered stay completely unchanged.
+    // The current native pack uses experts.bin; the GGUF fallback is also
+    // supported, without enabling cached reads during initial RAM/GPU fill.
+    auto r6_reopen_cached = [&] {
+        const char* r6_cache_env = std::getenv("STRATA_R6_FILE_READ_CACHE");
+        if (r6_cache_env != nullptr && std::atoi(r6_cache_env) == 1) {
+            std::vector<void*> original_handles = std::move(direct_);
+            direct_.clear();
+            std::vector<std::vector<uint8_t>> reference;
+            const int64_t layers[3] = {0, n_layers_ / 2, n_layers_ - 1};
+            const int64_t experts[3] = {0, n_expert_ / 2, n_expert_ - 1};
+            bool ok = n_layers_ > 0 && n_expert_ > 0;
+            // Read the reference with the original unbuffered handles.
+            direct_.swap(original_handles);
+            for (int i = 0; ok && i < 3; ++i) {
+                reference.emplace_back((size_t) layer_blob_bytes_[(size_t) layers[i]]);
+                const Fill f{0, layers[i], experts[i], reference.back().data()};
+                ok = !reference.back().empty() && read_direct(&f, 1);
+            }
+            direct_.swap(original_handles);
+            std::string reopen_why;
+            for (const std::string& path : paths_) {
+                if (!ok) break;
+                const int wide = MultiByteToWideChar(CP_UTF8, 0, path.c_str(), -1, nullptr, 0);
+                std::vector<wchar_t> w((size_t) (wide > 0 ? wide : 1), L'\0');
+                if (wide > 0) MultiByteToWideChar(CP_UTF8, 0, path.c_str(), -1, w.data(), wide);
+                HANDLE h = CreateFileW(w.data(), GENERIC_READ, FILE_SHARE_READ, nullptr, OPEN_EXISTING,
+                                       FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED, nullptr);
+                if (h == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE) {
+                    reopen_why = "cached CreateFileW error " + std::to_string((unsigned long long) GetLastError());
+                    ok = false;
+                    break;
+                }
+                direct_.push_back(h);
+            }
+            for (int i = 0; ok && i < 3; ++i) {
+                std::vector<uint8_t> check(reference[(size_t) i].size());
+                const Fill f{0, layers[i], experts[i], check.data()};
+                for (int repeat = 0; ok && repeat < 2; ++repeat) {
+                    ok = read_direct(&f, 1) &&
+                         std::memcmp(check.data(), reference[(size_t) i].data(), check.size()) == 0;
+                }
+            }
+            if (ok) {
+                for (void* h : original_handles) close_direct(h);
+                why += "; R6 Windows cached ReadFile active; flags=0x40000000; byte-probe PASS (3 blobs x 2 reads); source=";
+                why += maps_.empty() ? "experts.bin" : "GGUF shards";
+            } else {
+                for (void* h : direct_) close_direct(h);
+                direct_ = std::move(original_handles);
+                why += "; R6 cached ReadFile NOT activated; original handles restored: " + reopen_why;
+            }
+        }
+    };
     if (!role_ptr_.empty() || !maps_.empty()) {
         // the GGUF in place: nothing in the engine reads the shards through these views any more (the token embedding is
         // copied into pinned host memory at load, the dense weights and the head into VRAM, the PLE table has its own
@@ -1742,6 +1797,7 @@ bool FileExpertSource::drop_mapping(std::string& why) {
         std::snprintf(note, sizeof note, "%zu GGUF shard%s unmapped; every expert outside RAM and VRAM is read unbuffered",
                       maps_.size(), maps_.size() == 1 ? "" : "s");
         why = note;
+        r6_reopen_cached();
         return true;
     }
     if (base_ == nullptr || unmapped_) { why = base_ == nullptr ? "not open" : "already closed"; return unmapped_; }
@@ -1753,6 +1809,7 @@ bool FileExpertSource::drop_mapping(std::string& why) {
     if (file_ != nullptr) CloseHandle((HANDLE) file_);
     file_ = nullptr;
     why = "every expert outside RAM and VRAM is read unbuffered";
+    r6_reopen_cached();
     return true;
 #else
     // Linux's page cache has no such lock: only the tests close the GGUF views (STRATA_TEST_UNMAP_GGUF=1), to run the
```

Full experiment branch: https://github.com/durikim1-cpu/Strata/tree/rx7800xt-experiments

Engine 0.1.43.1 (a0116b2), same code in 0.1.44.1 and 0.1.45 (477ac13) (`include/strata/core/conversation_cache.hpp`, `drop_superseded`). Linux, 2x gfx906 layer split, `--conversation-cache-mib 20480 --conversation-cache-slots 4`, `--prompt-cache` at its default 6, `--prompt-cache-every` 16384. Client: pi (OpenAI chat completions, reasoning on, reasoning not sent back in the history).

Workflow: MAIN -> SUB (many turns) -> MAIN, as in #342. MAIN is evicted again once SUB's conversation is long.

What the log shows (one SUB conversation, MAIN parked):

```
prompt 82089 tokens = 81686 reused + 403 read ..., 2226 generated ..., 6 checkpoints
conversation cache: parked 84314 tokens in 21658.4 ms; parked=4 bytes=8650931864 evictions=37 ...
prompt 86065 tokens = 82084 reused + 3981 read ...          <- resumed at the turn boundary, 5 tokens before the previous prompt's end
prompt 88102 tokens = 88065 reused + 37 read ...            <- 5 continuation turns, each extends the live conversation
prompt 88491 tokens = 88454 reused + 37 read ...
prompt 89260 tokens = 89238 reused + 22 read ...
prompt 92558 tokens = 89497 reused + 3061 read ..., 4607 generated
conversation cache: parked 97165 tokens in 10264.4 ms; parked=4 bytes=9217013552 evictions=38 ...   <- no "dropped superseded copy" line
...
conversation cache: parked 191846 tokens in 24323.4 ms; parked=4 bytes=12335948288 evictions=41 ...
prompt 82329 tokens = 0 reused + 82329 read in 173952 ms    <- MAIN comes back, re-read from token 0
```

Every turn whose reply contained thinking is re-rendered by the client without it, so the next prompt diverges 5 tokens before the previous prompt's end, the engine resumes at the turn-boundary checkpoint and parks the outgoing state (the #342 case). `drop_superseded` then looks for that copy's deepest checkpoint in the outgoing chain by exact equality. On a long conversation the chain's 6 slots hold the root plus 4-5 periodic (16K) checkpoints, every continuation turn adds a turn-boundary checkpoint, and the LRU rotation drops the boundary the copy was parked at within a few turns. In the stretch above there were 5 continuation turns between the two parks (elsewhere 20+), so the copy is never recognised, `make_room` evicts oldest-first and MAIN goes after 4 such parks. The eviction counter climbs on every park (35 -> 41) with `parked=4` throughout.

Raising `--prompt-cache` does not fix it (20+ turns between parks). `--conversation-cache-min-tokens` does not apply (SUB is longer than MAIN).

Proposed fix: when the outgoing chain holds a checkpoint deeper than the copy's deepest one (the boundaries that pushed it out), the outgoing conversation *starting with* that checkpoint says the same thing as holding it. It continued from there, so the entry is this conversation a turn or more back:

```cpp
        auto held = [&](const ConversationCheckpoint& c) {
            if (c.ids == ids && c.imgs == images) return true;
            for (const auto& k : checkpoints)
                if (k.ids == c.ids && k.imgs == c.imgs) return true;
            // the chain rotated the boundary out: newer boundaries are in it and the ids still start with the old one
            bool deeper = false;
            for (const auto& k : checkpoints) deeper = deeper || k.ids.size() > c.ids.size();
            return deeper && conversation_prefix(c, ids, images) > 0;
        };
```

A sibling conversation that shares only the root (or a branch) has its own, deeper, boundary checkpoint which the outgoing ids do not start with, so it is kept. A chain with nothing deeper than the entry has not rotated anything, so the equality rule stays the whole rule there. All existing cases in `conversation_cache_test.cpp` still pass (including "ties prefer most recently parked" and the "what is NOT superseded" block). A test that reproduces the rotation (three continuation turns per park with a 3-slot chain) fails on the current header (`one parked copy of SUB at a time although its boundary rotated out`) and passes with the change. Running it patched here since 2026-10-14.

Side observation from the same run, maybe worth its own issue: with the mmap arena (`STRATA_ARENA_MMAP=1`) on a 64 GB box the parked snapshots (12.3 GB at the time) were paged out to swap because `--conversation-cache-min-free-mib` reads MemAvailable, which counts the experts.bin page cache as free; parking then took 20-28 s per switch and the watchdog ended the engine on a 60 s stall in "parking the previous conversation". A floor against anonymous RAM (MemAvailable minus the engine's own mapped file pages) would have refused those parks.

### What happened

With `--resident-budget-gib 40` on two RX 7900 XTX (layer split), the engine page-locks a 28.47 GiB cache complement
with one `cudaHostAlloc(Mapped | Portable)` (`FileExpertSource::pin_cache_complement`). On this machine that step
often takes 2-6 minutes, at other times 25-35 s, with the same config. Strata's own copy into the complement is 14-17 s every
time. The rest of the time goes to one call into the driver that fails and is retried thousands of times.

With transparent huge pages (THP) disabled for the server process (`prctl(PR_SET_THP_DISABLE)`, inherited by the
engine), the same start took 33-35 s in 4 of 4 starts, against 294-322 s for the default in 4 of 4 starts,
interleaved, same config, same hour. Decode and prompt speed did not change measurably.

#### Interleaved A/B, real engine (2026-10-16, 15:39-16:27)

Same config each time (below), back-to-back fresh starts, nothing else on the GPUs; the bench after each start is one
short prompt and one 32K prompt, 512 tokens generated at the server's default sampling (temperature 1.0), so the
tok/s columns are single requests. "alloc" = the complement total minus its copy: the allocation plus a few seconds of
bookkeeping.

| start | THP | complement total (s) | copy (s) | alloc (s) | load to ready (s) | gen short | gen 32K | prompt 32K |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| d1 | default | 300 | 14 | 286 | 370 | 68.0 | 70.5 | 1955 |
| n1 | off | 35 | 15 | 20 | 77 | 67.4 | 68.2 | 1912 |
| d2 | default | 301 | 14 | 287 | 363 | 67.7 | 61.8 | 1570 |
| n2 | off | 33 | 15 | 18 | 75 | 40.0 | 72.2 | 1902 |
| d3 | default | 294 | 14 | 280 | 356 | 77.6 | 71.4 | 1812 |
| n3 | off | 33 | 15 | 18 | 73 | 74.8 | 80.9 | 1918 |
| d4 | default | 322 | 15 | 307 | 370 | 67.5 | 65.6 | 1900 |
| n4 | off | 35 | 17 | 18 | 74 | 67.1 | 72.6 | 1972 |
| **median, default (4)** | | **300.5** | 14 | **286.5** | **366.5** | 67.8 | 68.0 | 1856 |
| **median, THP off (4)** | | **34.0** | 15 | **18.0** | **74.5** | 67.2 | 72.4 | 1915 |

Two more default starts were run under strace (`strace -f --seccomp-bpf -T -tt -e trace=ioctl`): 91 s (its copy took
73 s that time, the allocation 8.5 s with 93 retries) and 223 s (below). Before the test, in normal use the same config
started 8 times on 2026-10-16 with a complement time of 174 (that one took the paced #1250 path), 33, 132, 376, 347, 316, 38 and 386 s. Over 76 starts in
our logs since 2026-10-12 (0.1.43.1 and 0.1.44.1) the median is 27 s and the slow ones are 75-497 s: many
calibration starts in a row were fast; in normal use (a coding benchmark between starts) most are slow.

#### Where the time goes

ROCr implements this `hipHostMalloc` as an anonymous `mmap` of the whole size, `madvise(MADV_HUGEPAGE)` on it, then one
`AMDKFD_IOC_ALLOC_MEMORY_OF_GPU` (a KFD userptr: `/proc/<pid>/status` shows `VmLck` and `VmPin` 0 after it). From the
standalone test's trace:

```
mmap(NULL, 30571532288, PROT_NONE, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_NORESERVE, -1, 0) = 0x7c4843cb7000
mmap(0x7c4843e00000, 30569431040, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_FIXED|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0) = 0x7c4843e00000
madvise(0x7c4843e00000, 30569431040, MADV_HUGEPAGE) = 0
ioctl(3, AMDKFD_IOC_ALLOC_MEMORY_OF_GPU, 0x7ffedcee90e0) = -1 EAGAIN (Resource temporarily unavailable) <2.336246>
ioctl(3, AMDKFD_IOC_ALLOC_MEMORY_OF_GPU, 0x7ffedcee90e0) = -1 EAGAIN (Resource temporarily unavailable) <0.063404>
... (the same call again, thousands of times)
```

In the real engine's 223 s start, the complement's allocation call was made **about 3,770 times from 16:20:09.8 to
16:23:28.4 (198.6 s)**, every attempt but the last returning EAGAIN; the last succeeded (0.34 s) and the GPU mapping after it took
0.29 s. In a slow start in normal use (sampled from `/proc`, no strace), one engine thread
was at 100% CPU for 5 minutes, all of it system time (`wchan` 0), while the process's resident memory grew by about
95 MB/s; the 28 GiB landed in the last ~20 s.

#### Standalone reproduction (no model, no kernels)

`pintest.cpp` (below) makes the same call (`hipHostMalloc(28.47 GiB, Mapped | Portable)`) and times it, with
`/proc/vmstat` deltas over the allocation:

| case | alloc | notes |
|---|---:|---|
| right after a real engine exited (item 1067, ~30 min of serving), THP default, under strace | **1071 s** | ~11,000 EAGAIN (1,069 s inside the failing calls); `pgfault` +31.2M for 7.46M pages; `thp_fault_alloc` +14,698; `compact_stall` +1,567; `pswpin` +257,614 |
| same call under the same strace, later | 2.9 s | 0 EAGAIN (so strace is not what makes it slow) |
| THP default, idle machine (several runs) | 2.9-3.2 s | `pgfault` = pages, compaction ~0 |
| THP default, page cache full of a 30 GB file | 6.9 s | `compact_stall` +2,538 |
| THP off (`PR_SET_THP_DISABLE`), idle / cache full / cache dropped / after a 28 GiB pinned process exits | 8.2 s each | no compaction at all |
| `malloc` + `memset(0)` + `hipHostRegister(Mapped \| Portable)` | 9.2 s | the compiler made it a calloc (memset 0.00 s), so the pages were faulted inside `hipHostRegister` |
| 15 pieces of 2 GiB | 3.6 s | |
| one visible GPU (`HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0`) | 3.1 s | |

Only the first row hit the slow state. I could not bring it on with a standalone recipe (a full page cache, a dropped
cache, a large pinned process that just exited). Today's back-to-back real starts with the default were slow 5 of 6
times (the A/B's 4, the 223 s traced one; the other traced one was 91 s), but in a calibration run the night before,
many back-to-back starts of the same config were fast; I do not know what differs. So the
standalone rows other than the first show only the cost of each variant **in the fast state**. THP off is the only
variant measured in the slow state (the A/B above).

#### What I think happens (not confirmed: no root tracing on this machine)

The userptr allocation walks the range with HMM (`hmm_range_fault`) and returns `-EAGAIN` when the range is
invalidated during the walk; ROCr then retries the whole 28.47 GiB. With THP `enabled=madvise`, `defrag=madvise`, the
2 MB faults in this `MADV_HUGEPAGE` region can do direct compaction and reclaim, which migrate movable pages, possibly
pages of this same range faulted in a moment earlier, and every move invalidates the walk. That fits the slow
standalone run (each page faulted ~4 times; huge pages were allocated for the whole range, yet with 22 GiB of it
resident only ~5 GiB of the machine's anonymous memory was in huge pages; compaction stalls) and the fast THP-off runs (one fault per page, no compaction), but I have not traced the
invalidations. It may be the same family as the reclaim / migration + KFD userptr stalls in `docs/AMD_HIP.md`
(#750, #1705, #2413). We also saw one `verify: timed out at layer 32 (#267)` on 0.1.44.1 the same day, but
I have not tested whether THP off changes those.

#### Workaround that works here

Start the server with THP disabled for it; the engine inherits it:

```c
// nothp.c - gcc -O2 -o nothp nothp.c ; usage: nothp <command> [args...]
#include <stdio.h>
#include <sys/prctl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    if (argc < 2) { fprintf(stderr, "usage: nothp <command> [args...]\n"); return 2; }
    if (prctl(PR_SET_THP_DISABLE, 1, 0, 0, 0) != 0) { perror("prctl(PR_SET_THP_DISABLE)"); return 1; }
    execvp(argv[1], argv + 1);
    perror("execvp");
    return 127;
}
```

`nothp python serve/server.py --engine strata --config <config.json>`. No root needed. Cost: about 5 s more for the
allocation in the fast state (8.2 s against 3 s); no measurable change in decode or prompt speed in the A/B above
(4 starts per arm, single requests, so only large differences would show).

#### Possible changes in Strata (suggestions; only the first is measured, and only as the process-wide workaround)

1. Disable THP only around the complement's allocation: `prctl(PR_SET_THP_DISABLE, 1)` before the `cudaHostAlloc`,
   restore the previous value after it. The faults happen inside that call. (Other threads' faults in that window
   lose THP too.)
2. Allocate the complement in 2 GiB pieces, as #2485 does for SYCL: 3.6 s in the fast state; not measured in the slow
   state, where a piece that is invalidated would retry only its own 2 GiB.
3. Print the allocation time separately from the copy in the `complement ready` line, so a slow start can be told
   apart from a slow read.

#### Not tested

System-wide `transparent_hugepage/defrag` = `defer+madvise` or `never`, or `enabled=never` (root); a kernel trace of
the invalidations (root); other ROCm or kernel versions; one GPU with the real engine; `HSA_USERPTR_FOR_PAGED_MEM=0`
(as far as I can tell it applies to paged allocations, not this one); what makes back-to-back starts slow on one day and fast
on another (our workload between starts is the obvious suspect).

<details><summary>pintest.cpp (standalone timing)</summary>

```cpp
// 2026-10-16: standalone timing of the page-locked host allocation Strata 0.1.44.1 makes for its RAM expert complement
// (FileExpertSource::pin_cache_complement: one cudaHostAlloc(bytes, Mapped|Portable) -> hipHostMalloc on ROCm), to
// show whether its 33-386 s spread is in the HIP/KFD allocation itself. No model, no kernels.
//
// Usage: pintest <mode> <GiB> [chunk_GiB] [repeats]
//   mode  alloc-mp   hipHostMalloc(Mapped|Portable)            - what Strata does
//         alloc-m    hipHostMalloc(Mapped)                     - one GPU's mapping only
//         alloc-d    hipHostMalloc(Default)
//         register   malloc + memset(0) + hipHostRegister(Mapped|Portable); the compiler may turn malloc + memset(0)
//                    into calloc (it did here: 0.00 s), so the pages are faulted inside hipHostRegister
//   chunk_GiB  0 = one allocation (default); N = ceil(GiB/N) allocations of N GiB
// Per repeat: the alloc time, then a one-thread memset of every byte (Strata copies the experts in next), then the free.
// /proc/vmstat counters (compaction, direct reclaim, swap, THP) are printed as deltas over the alloc.
#include <hip/hip_runtime.h>
#include <chrono>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <cstring>
#include <fstream>
#include <map>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

static double now_s() {
    return std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now().time_since_epoch()).count();
}

static std::map<std::string, long long> vmstat() {
    static const char* keys[] = {"compact_stall", "compact_fail", "compact_success", "pgsteal_direct", "pgscan_direct",
                                 "allocstall_normal", "allocstall_movable", "pswpin", "pswpout", "thp_fault_alloc",
                                 "pgmajfault", "pgfault", "nr_free_pages", "thp_split_page", "thp_fault_fallback", nullptr};
    std::map<std::string, long long> m;
    std::ifstream f("/proc/vmstat");
    std::string k;
    long long v;
    while (f >> k >> v)
        for (const char** p = keys; *p; ++p)
            if (k == *p) m[k] = v;
    return m;
}

static std::string vmdelta(const std::map<std::string, long long>& a, const std::map<std::string, long long>& b) {
    std::ostringstream o;
    for (auto& [k, v] : b) {
        long long d = v - a.at(k);
        if (d != 0) o << " " << k << (d > 0 ? "+" : "") << d;
    }
    return o.str();
}

static long long meminfo_kib(const char* key) {
    std::ifstream f("/proc/meminfo");
